\title{What `ensemble` means, and when to change it}
\description{
In the LFENM there are no amino acids. A mutation at site \code{j} is
represented directly as a set of random perturbations applied to the
equilibrium lengths of that site's contacts — not as the substitution of one
residue type for another. Nothing in the model says which amino acid a
mutation *is*, and \code{mutation} is not drawn from a finite set: there are
infinitely many possible mutations at every site.

That leaves a gap. A real substitution has an identity: measuring the same
one twice must give the same answer. \code{ensemble} is what fills it.
Together, \code{ensemble}, \code{site_mut} and \code{mutation} determine the
perturbations completely — with \code{ensemble} held fixed, "mutation 3 at
site 80" always means the same set of contact perturbations, in every
session and every script.

So \code{ensemble} names **which realization of the mutational process** you
are working in. Changing it does not add randomness or draw again: it moves
you to a different realization, in which "mutation 3 at site 80" is a
different mutation.

Note what the realization does *not* control. Which contacts a mutation
perturbs is fixed by the site and by \code{mut_sd_min} — it is a property of
the contact map. Only the magnitudes of the perturbations vary between
realizations.
}
\section{Within one experiment, hold it fixed}{


A mutational scan, an evolutionary trajectory, a set of results compared
against each other — all must run under a single \code{ensemble}.

Varying it partway through is not a way of getting more randomness. It
switches realization, so that mutant \code{(80, 3)} early in the run and
mutant \code{(80, 3)} later are different mutations wearing the same name.
The results are then not comparable, and nothing in the output records that
it happened.
}

\section{Between experiments, it is a knob}{


Rerunning an analysis under a second \code{ensemble} asks a real question:
does the conclusion survive a different realization, or was it an artifact
of this one? That is the intended use.

Two values also give two statistically independent replicate ensembles —
what a double-mutant scan needs. Over a 228-site, 10-mutation scan, no two
mutants drawn under different \code{ensemble} values share a random stream,
including for adjacent values such as 1 and 2.
}

\section{How many mutations you ask for does not matter}{


The key never mentions \code{nmut}, so a scan of 50 mutations per site
contains the 10-mutation scan exactly: the first ten mutants at each site
are the same ten mutants. A scan can be extended without invalidating what
you already have.
}

\section{Choosing a value}{


Any integer will do; no realization is better than another. What matters is
that the value is recorded alongside the results — without it they cannot be
regenerated, and a mutant identified as \code{(80, 3)} means nothing.

\code{get_mutant_site()} defaults to \code{ensemble = 1L}, the first
realization, so that examples and casual exploration work. Pass it
explicitly in analysis code, so the realization is recorded in your script
rather than in whichever version of penm happened to be installed.
}

\section{How it works}{


The tuple \code{(ensemble, site_mut, mutation)} is hashed, and the hash
seeds the RNG for that one mutant's perturbations. \code{ensemble} is
therefore not a seed in the \code{set.seed()} sense — it is a label, and the
seed is derived from it.

Hashing, rather than arithmetic, is what keeps distinct mutants distinct. An
arithmetic key such as \code{seed + site_mut * mutation} maps every divisor
pair of the same product onto one stream, which silently made thousands of
mutants in a scan copies of each other. Drawing each mutant from its own
hashed stream is also why a scan does not depend on \code{nmut}, and why
mutants can be generated in any order.
}

\examples{
wt <- set_enm(pdb_2acy_A, node = "ca", model = "ming_wall",
              d_max = 10.5, frustrated = FALSE)

# Same realization, same (site, mutation): the same mutation, twice.
a <- get_mutant_site(wt, site_mut = 80, mutation = 3, ensemble = 1L)
b <- get_mutant_site(wt, site_mut = 80, mutation = 3, ensemble = 1L)
identical(a$graph$lij, b$graph$lij)

# A different realization: "mutation 3 at site 80" is now something else.
c <- get_mutant_site(wt, site_mut = 80, mutation = 3, ensemble = 2L)
identical(a$graph$lij, c$graph$lij)

# ... but it perturbs the same contacts. Which edges are touched is a
# property of the site, not of the realization.
identical(which(a$graph$lij != wt$graph$lij),
          which(c$graph$lij != wt$graph$lij))

}
